 "transcript": {
  "answer": "It's not just you, but the data suggests your bill isn't big because of a recent price jump.\n\n**Perth electricity prices (ABS CPI, year-on-year to August 2026)**\n- Perth electricity rose **2.4%**. The July 2026 tariff change added 2.4%, and the index has been flat since.\n- The national average (eight capitals) rose **13.2%**, so Perth's rise is much smaller.\n- Perth's overall CPI rose **4.3%**, against **4.0%** nationally. Electricity isn't what's pushing Perth's inflation up.\n\n**Why Perth's numbers swing so much**\n- The Perth electricity index (September 2025 = 100) was about 26 in January–February 2025. It reached 100 by July 2025 and dropped to about 69 in November–December 2025. It was back at 100 by February 2026 and is now 102.4.\n- That pattern looks like government energy bill credits being applied and then expiring, not real changes in the tariff. The data doesn't say this directly, so it's my inference.\n- If your credits ended, your bill could have jumped even though the underlying price barely moved.\n\n**Why your bill may still feel huge**\n- The index measures price, not usage. Summer air-conditioning in Perth can push a bill up a lot.\n- Perth's price level is now about 77% above early 2025. That is mostly because the credits ended, as explained above.\n- Prices are up across the country. Nationally the index is about 27% above January 2025, and household budgets are tight with headline inflation around 4%.\n\nIf you tell me whether your last bill covered summer, or whether you had a credit before, I can help work out which factor matters most for you.",
